{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.eeeec62d24","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-288","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Pembrook Tower is in eastern Australia. It was completed in 1930.</li><li>Its height is 296 meters.</li><li>The Juniper Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1994.</li><li>Its height is 293 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Pembrook Tower and the Juniper Tower.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Pembrook Tower is in eastern Australia, and the Juniper Tower is in central Canada.","The height of the Pembrook Tower (296 meters) is greater than that of the Juniper Tower (293 meters).","The Pembrook Tower was completed in 1930, while the Juniper Tower was completed in 1994.","Both the Pembrook Tower and the Juniper Tower are towers built mainly of steel."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7b0292d1d6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-290","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Valewood Tower is in southern New Zealand. It was completed in 1916.</li><li>Its height is 394 meters.</li><li>The Quarry Tower is in northern Chile. It was completed in 1961.</li><li>Its height is 123 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two towers were completed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Valewood Tower was completed in 1916, while the Quarry Tower was completed in 1961.","Both the Valewood Tower and the Quarry Tower are observation towers open to the public.","The Valewood Tower is in southern New Zealand, and the Quarry Tower is in northern Chile.","The height of the Valewood Tower (394 meters) is greater than that of the Quarry Tower (123 meters)."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.67927b4a6f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-300","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Fenwick Museum is in northern India. It opened in 2018.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 615,896 visitors.</li><li>The Ironwood Museum is in northern Japan. It opened in 1893.</li><li>Its annual attendance is 463,041 visitors.</li><li>Both are art museums with free admission.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the annual attendance of the two museums differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Fenwick Museum and the Ironwood Museum are art museums with free admission.","The annual attendance of the Fenwick Museum (615,896 visitors) is greater than that of the Ironwood Museum (463,041 visitors).","The Fenwick Museum, which is in northern India, is one of several art museums with free admission.","The Fenwick Museum opened in 2018, while the Ironwood Museum opened in 1893."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the annual attendance of both museums. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one museum."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.d9edbee718","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-314","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Thornbury Tower is in southern Brazil. It was completed in 1939.</li><li>Its height is 433 meters.</li><li>The Glenrock Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1903.</li><li>Its height is 302 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Thornbury Tower and the Glenrock Tower.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Thornbury Tower was completed in 1939, while the Glenrock Tower was completed in 1903.","The height of the Thornbury Tower (433 meters) is greater than that of the Glenrock Tower (302 meters).","The Thornbury Tower is in southern Brazil, and the Glenrock Tower is in central Canada.","Both the Thornbury Tower and the Glenrock Tower are towers built mainly of steel."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.21ac41408a","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-334","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Dunmore Tower is in eastern Australia. It was completed in 1943.</li><li>Its height is 503 meters.</li><li>The Larkspur Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1971.</li><li>Its height is 116 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the Dunmore Tower and the Larkspur Tower.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The height of the Dunmore Tower (503 meters) is greater than that of the Larkspur Tower (116 meters).","The Dunmore Tower is in eastern Australia, and the Larkspur Tower is in northern India.","The Dunmore Tower was completed in 1943, while the Larkspur Tower was completed in 1971.","Both the Dunmore Tower and the Larkspur Tower are towers built mainly of steel."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2de98dcdcb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-341","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Northam Tunnel is in western Scotland. It opened in 1924.</li><li>Its length is 45 kilometers.</li><li>The Halden Tunnel is in southern Norway. It opened in 1894.</li><li>Its length is 23 kilometers.</li><li>Both are railway tunnels.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two tunnels opened.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The length of the Northam Tunnel (45 kilometers) is greater than that of the Halden Tunnel (23 kilometers).","The Northam Tunnel is in western Scotland, and the Halden Tunnel is in southern Norway.","Both the Northam Tunnel and the Halden Tunnel are railway tunnels.","The Northam Tunnel opened in 1924, while the Halden Tunnel opened in 1894."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.966224ea88","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-350","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dunmore National Park is in northern Japan. It was established in 1959.</li><li>Its area is 8,377 square kilometers.</li><li>Redfern National Park is in southern Norway. It was established in 1952.</li><li>Its area is 3,713 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are parks known for their old-growth forests.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Dunmore National Park and Redfern National Park.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both Dunmore National Park and Redfern National Park are parks known for their old-growth forests.","The area of Dunmore National Park (8,377 square kilometers) is greater than that of Redfern National Park (3,713 square kilometers).","Dunmore National Park is in northern Japan, and Redfern National Park is in southern Norway.","Dunmore National Park was established in 1959, while Redfern National Park was established in 1952."],"answer":"A","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. Only this choice describes something the two share. The others present differences (in size, date, or location)."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.556f5c8cde","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-365","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Sable Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 2019.</li><li>Its height is 431 meters.</li><li>The Pembrook Tower is in central Spain. It was completed in 2003.</li><li>Its height is 382 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two towers were completed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Both the Sable Tower and the Pembrook Tower are observation towers open to the public.","The height of the Sable Tower (431 meters) is greater than that of the Pembrook Tower (382 meters).","The Sable Tower was completed in 2019, while the Pembrook Tower was completed in 2003.","The Sable Tower is in western Kenya, and the Pembrook Tower is in central Spain."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.2f2a5079f0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-366","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Pembrook Observatory is in southern New Zealand. It began operating in 1905.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 10 meters.</li><li>The Orla Observatory is in western Kenya. It began operating in 1926.</li><li>Its main telescope's mirror diameter is 8 meters.</li><li>Both are radio observatories.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two observatorys began operating.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The main telescope's mirror diameter of the Pembrook Observatory (10 meters) is greater than that of the Orla Observatory (8 meters).","The Pembrook Observatory began operating in 1905, while the Orla Observatory began operating in 1926.","The Pembrook Observatory is in southern New Zealand, and the Orla Observatory is in western Kenya.","Both the Pembrook Observatory and the Orla Observatory are radio observatories."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e06df70b65","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-374","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Merrow Tower is in western Kenya. It was completed in 1910.</li><li>Its height is 471 meters.</li><li>The Quarry Tower is in eastern Australia. It was completed in 1900.</li><li>Its height is 116 meters.</li><li>Both are observation towers open to the public.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast the locations of the two towers.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Merrow Tower was completed in 1910, while the Quarry Tower was completed in 1900.","The Merrow Tower, which is in western Kenya, is one of several observation towers open to the public.","The height of the Merrow Tower (471 meters) is greater than that of the Quarry Tower (116 meters).","The Merrow Tower is in western Kenya, and the Quarry Tower is in eastern Australia."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It names where both are and contrasts them. The other choices compare dates or size, or mention only one location."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.20d9fd587f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-384","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Larkspur is in western Scotland. It was first surveyed in 1899.</li><li>Its surface area is 523 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Brookhaven is in southern Norway. It was first surveyed in 1912.</li><li>Its surface area is 394 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Larkspur, which is in western Scotland, is one of several reservoirs created by dams.","Lake Larkspur was first surveyed in 1899, while Lake Brookhaven was first surveyed in 1912.","The surface area of Lake Larkspur (523 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Brookhaven (394 square kilometers).","Both Lake Larkspur and Lake Brookhaven are reservoirs created by dams."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9f24a2948b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-c-385","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The Wexford Tower is in central Canada. It was completed in 1928.</li><li>Its height is 348 meters.</li><li>The Northam Tower is in northern India. It was completed in 1925.</li><li>Its height is 195 meters.</li><li>Both are towers built mainly of steel.</li></ul><p>The student wants to contrast when the two towers were completed.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The Wexford Tower is in central Canada, and the Northam Tower is in northern India.","The height of the Wexford Tower (348 meters) is greater than that of the Northam Tower (195 meters).","Both the Wexford Tower and the Northam Tower are towers built mainly of steel.","The Wexford Tower was completed in 1928, while the Northam Tower was completed in 1925."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It gives both dates and contrasts them. The other choices compare size or location, or describe a similarity."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.07e393bad3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-1","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Omar Brennan is a marine biologist.</li><li>Brennan wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Brennan recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Brennan found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Brennan concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Brennan concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","Because whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often, Brennan concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","To investigate the question, Brennan recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Brennan found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b8a7cffce9","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-2","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Rahman is a marine biologist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Rahman recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Rahman found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Rahman concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Rahman recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Rahman concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","Marine biologist Jonas Rahman set out to learn whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.","Rahman found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.22108f67c0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-10","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Iyer is an ecologist.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning.</li><li>Iyer compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.</li><li>Iyer found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.</li><li>Iyer concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Iyer compared the first songs of robins in brightly lit city parks with those in dark rural woodlands.","Iyer concluded that artificial light may shift birds' daily rhythms.","Iyer found that robins in lit parks began singing nearly an hour earlier.","Ecologist Kofi Iyer set out to learn whether city lights change when songbirds begin singing in the morning."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.148611d370","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-16","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Haddad is a psychologist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Haddad had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Haddad found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Haddad concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Haddad concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","Haddad found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","Psychologist Felipe Haddad set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.","To investigate the question, Haddad had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3449cad7c1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-20","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Nakamura is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Nakamura grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Nakamura found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Nakamura concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","Agricultural scientist Dmitri Nakamura set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","Nakamura found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.","To investigate the question, Nakamura grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1cd37ddc77","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-21","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Samuel Rahman is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Rahman grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Rahman found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Rahman concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Agricultural scientist Samuel Rahman set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","Rahman concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","To investigate the question, Rahman grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.","Rahman found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9b27f09c00","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-26","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Moreau is a materials scientist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer.</li><li>Moreau coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.</li><li>Moreau found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.</li><li>Moreau concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau concluded that the coating could help reduce food waste.","Moreau found that coated strawberries stayed fresh for about four days longer.","To investigate the question, Moreau coated half of a batch of strawberries with a seaweed-based film and left the rest uncoated.","Materials scientist Kofi Moreau set out to learn whether a coating made from seaweed can keep fruit fresh longer."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.874d9e7575","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-31","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Asante is an entomologist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Asante set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Asante found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Asante set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Asante found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","Entomologist Yara Asante set out to learn whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.","Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.28a5c364af","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-32","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Omar Asante is an entomologist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether bees prefer flowers of a particular color.</li><li>Asante set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.</li><li>Asante found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.</li><li>Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers.","To investigate the question, Asante set out identical feeders colored blue, yellow, and white in a meadow.","Asante found that bees visited the blue feeders most often.","Because bees visited the blue feeders most often, Asante concluded that color may play a role in how bees choose flowers."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.53b8265a35","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-36","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Asante is a sociologist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another.</li><li>Asante surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.</li><li>Asante found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.</li><li>Asante concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Asante surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.","Asante found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.","Asante concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.","Sociologist Leila Asante set out to learn whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.516727f98b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-43","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Amara Halvorsen is a geologist.</li><li>Halvorsen wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Halvorsen measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Halvorsen found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Halvorsen concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Halvorsen found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","Halvorsen concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","To investigate the question, Halvorsen measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Geologist Amara Halvorsen set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.34074c6afd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-44","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Takeda is a geologist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Takeda measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Takeda found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Geologist Ravi Takeda set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.","Takeda found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","To investigate the question, Takeda measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Takeda concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.cab5fd4d8b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-46","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Okafor is a geologist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Okafor measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Okafor found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Okafor concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Okafor measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.","Because bare slopes lost several times more soil, Okafor concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","Okafor found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","Okafor concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a4b306ce26","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-67","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Petrov is a linguist.</li><li>Petrov wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Petrov gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Petrov found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Petrov concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Petrov gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Petrov found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","Petrov concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.","Because bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors, Petrov concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.4a968143f9","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-69","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Varga is a linguist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.</li><li>Varga gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.</li><li>Varga found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.</li><li>Varga concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Linguist Felipe Varga set out to learn whether bilingual children are better at switching between tasks.","To investigate the question, Varga gave bilingual and monolingual children a game that required switching rules quickly.","Varga found that bilingual children switched rules with fewer errors.","Varga concluded that speaking two languages may strengthen certain mental skills."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1c91b19888","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-80","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Varga is a botanist.</li><li>Varga wanted to find out whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.</li><li>Varga grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.</li><li>Varga found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.</li><li>Varga concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Varga grew tomato plants in two identical greenhouses, one with music and one without.","Varga found that plants in both greenhouses grew at similar rates.","Botanist Kenji Varga set out to learn whether tomato plants grow better with music played nearby.","Varga concluded that music may have little effect on tomato growth."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.5cf7f3ce50","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-85","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Imani Oyelaran is an urban planner.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.","Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.","Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","Urban planner Imani Oyelaran set out to learn whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9c344838d3","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-85","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Imani Oyelaran is an urban planner.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.","Because streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler, Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.","Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.32618089b0","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-87","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kwame Mensah is an urban planner.</li><li>Mensah wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Mensah measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Mensah found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Mensah concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Urban planner Kwame Mensah set out to learn whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.","Mensah concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","To investigate the question, Mensah measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.","Mensah found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.8f0267338c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-121","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Ferreira is an ornithologist.</li><li>Ferreira wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Ferreira measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Ferreira found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Ferreira measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.","Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.","Ferreira found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.","Because species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings, Ferreira concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.e1d2c44f5f","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-138","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kwame Okafor is a neuroscientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.</li><li>Okafor scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.</li><li>Okafor found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.</li><li>Okafor concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.","Okafor found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.","Neuroscientist Kwame Okafor set out to learn whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.","To investigate the question, Okafor scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.83a02c1b8c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-149","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Kowalski is a marine ecologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether sea urchins limit the growth of kelp forests.</li><li>Kowalski fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open.</li><li>Kowalski found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kowalski found that kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser.","Because kelp in the fenced plots grew much denser, Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","Kowalski concluded that urchins may keep kelp forests from expanding.","To investigate the question, Kowalski fenced urchins out of some kelp plots and left others open."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bfa7e94d16","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-164","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lucía Kowalski is a veterinary scientist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether dogs recognize their owners' voices.</li><li>Kowalski played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.</li><li>Kowalski found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Kowalski played recordings of owners' voices and strangers' voices to dogs.","Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.","Because dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often, Kowalski concluded that dogs may be able to tell their owners' voices apart from others.","Kowalski found that dogs turned toward their owners' voices far more often."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.db59350bf2","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-172","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kenji Oyelaran is a forest ecologist.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether fungi help trees share nutrients.</li><li>Oyelaran added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.</li><li>Oyelaran found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Oyelaran concluded that fungal networks may move nutrients between trees.","To investigate the question, Oyelaran added a traceable form of carbon to some trees and checked nearby trees for it.","Forest ecologist Kenji Oyelaran set out to learn whether fungi help trees share nutrients.","Oyelaran found that the carbon appeared in neighboring trees connected by fungal networks."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b0088c4c09","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-176","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Lindqvist is a music researcher.</li><li>Lindqvist wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Lindqvist tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Lindqvist found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Lindqvist concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lindqvist found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","To investigate the question, Lindqvist tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Because children who took lessons improved more than those who did not, Lindqvist concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.","Lindqvist concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.94cdfac9d7","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"group":"syn-s-177","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Zara Vance is a music researcher.</li><li>Vance wanted to find out whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.</li><li>Vance tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.</li><li>Vance found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.</li><li>Vance concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Music researcher Zara Vance set out to learn whether learning an instrument improves children's listening skills.","Vance found that children who took lessons improved more than those who did not.","To investigate the question, Vance tested children's ability to pick out sounds in noise before and after two years of music lessons.","Vance concluded that music training may sharpen listening skills."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."}
